  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Given that the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{2}-6}{c{x}^{2}+32x+8}$ is $y=\\frac{1}{4},$ find the value of $c.$",
  "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}-6}{c{x}^{2}+32x+8} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{c{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{32x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(5-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}})}{(c+\\frac{32}{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-0}{c+0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{c} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ c & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
